.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:2:name: executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:3:description: Worktree canonical files (skills/, agents/) are branch-isolated copies on the worktree branch — executors MUST use the worktree-absolute path to edit them; bare `.gobbi/projects/...` resolves to the main tree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:9:session: 1b26cf20-677b-498c-8c1b-7d7e971597ac
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:10:tags: [process, worktree, execution]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:16:# Worktree canonical files are branch-isolated — always use the worktree-absolute path
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:18:When a worktree is checked out, `git` materializes the branch into the worktree directory. The canonical `.gobbi/projects/{project}/skills/` and `agents/` files are **branch-isolated copies on the worktree's branch** — they are NOT shared with the main tree. If an executor edits the bare path `.gobbi/projects/...` from a cwd that resolves to the main tree, the worktree branch remains untouched. The executor must use the **worktree-absolute path** (e.g., `<worktree-root>/.gobbi/projects/{project}/skills/{skill}/SKILL.md`) for every file touched in a worktree session.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:20:> **Separate concern:** the `.claude/skills/` mirror is per-file SYMLINKS into the canonical `.gobbi/.../skills/` tree (not physical copies). One edit to the canonical file reflects through the symlink automatically — no double edit needed. See `mistakes/skills-mirror-symlinks-not-copies.md` for that distinct concern.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:24:In worktree mode (the canonical mode this session shipped via T1), the worktree's `.gobbi/projects/{project}/skills/...` files are **branch-isolated physical copies on the worktree branch** — each worktree checkout materializes its own copy of these files on its branch, not symlinks back to the main tree. Multiple executors this session were briefed with the older guidance "edit the canonical mirror at `.gobbi/projects/...`" without absolute paths, then proceeded to edit the main-tree copy (because their cwd resolved there) — leaving the worktree branch untouched. Task 03 surfaced this explicitly: the executor reported editing main-tree canonical, then had to revert + re-apply inside the worktree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:26:The corrective stance: worktree-mode delegation prompts MUST specify the canonical path as the **worktree-absolute** path (e.g., `<worktree>/.gobbi/projects/{project}/skills/{skill}/SKILL.md`), not the bare `.gobbi/projects/...` form. The executor's `cd` (or absolute path use) must be inside the worktree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:30:- The whole point of worktree mode is branch-isolated changes; editing the main-tree copy defeats it, so a bare-path edit silently subverts the worktree's purpose.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:31:- The Edit-tool symlink-refusal mistake (`edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md`) already pushes executors to the canonical-mirror path — without absolute disambiguation the fallback path is ambiguous between worktree and main tree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:36:- Trust the executor to `cd` into the worktree first. Rejected — multiple briefs this session showed executors editing main-tree even when told to `cd`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:41:- Manager delegation prompts in worktree mode need a "Worktree-canonical paths" block calling out the absolute path.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:42:- The phase-doc per-iter commit cadence rule (T05) is unaffected — it already references `worktreePath`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:46:Add a "Canonical-mirror paths (worktree-absolute)" subsection to executor briefs in worktree mode, listing every file the executor may touch as `<worktree>/.gobbi/projects/{project}/skills/...`. Executors should use absolute paths throughout — never bare `.gobbi/projects/...` in a worktree session.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:50:- Brief uses bare `.gobbi/projects/...` for an executor without an explicit absolute path or a `cd "$worktreePath"` instruction.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:51:- Executor's verification command reads from main-tree rather than `git -C <worktree> show HEAD:<path>`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:52:- An executor reports "edited `.gobbi/projects/...`" without an absolute worktree prefix.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:56:- `mistakes/skills-mirror-symlinks-not-copies.md` — the `.claude/skills/` mirror is symlinks, not physical copies; editing the canonical file reflects automatically. That is a SEPARATE concern from worktree branch-isolation described here.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-mirror-path-vs-worktree-physical-copy.md:57:- [[edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths]] — sibling mistake covering the Edit-tool symlink-refusal fallback that motivates the canonical-mirror path guidance.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:2:name: evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:3:description: "Claude evaluator returned verdict inline; did not write the contracted per-perspective files when the Write tool was absent."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:9:session: 7ea62d36-e826-4ce6-9e90-9e948007b068
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:10:tags: [process, evaluation, orchestration]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:16:# Evaluator returns verdict inline; no per-perspective files written
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:20:T05 Claude evaluator (iter1) returned a PASS verdict with full per-perspective findings inline in its response. It did NOT write the contracted 8 per-perspective .md files (project / structure / performance / aesthetics / usage / consistency / risk / overall) to the evaluation output dir despite the brief explicitly requiring those output paths.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:22:Manager wrote a proxy overall.md to preserve the verdict and 2 Low findings to disk for audit-trail continuity, but project / structure / performance / aesthetics / usage / consistency / risk per-perspective files are missing from `sessions/.../execution/T5/evaluation/iter1/claude/`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:26:The `evaluator` agent type's `tools` list per `.claude/agents/evaluator.md` is `Read, Grep, Glob, Bash` — **no `Write` tool**. The evaluator interpreted this absence (plus possibly some directive in the agent system prompt to "not write report .md files") as forbidding file writes, and chose to return findings inline.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:28:This is BOTH a tools issue (no Write means writes must go via Bash heredoc) AND a behavioral issue (the evaluator chose inline rather than `cat > file.md << EOF` via Bash). Prior evaluators in this session DID write files (via Bash heredoc); this one chose differently.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:36:2. **Make Bash-heredoc-write explicit in the evaluator brief**. Add a line: "If you lack the Write tool, use `mkdir -p <dir> && cat > <path> << 'EOF' ... EOF` via Bash for each of the 8 per-perspective files. Do NOT return findings inline — the per-perspective files are the audit trail."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:38:3. **Add a verification gate to the manager's post-evaluation step**: after evaluator returns DONE, manager runs `ls -1 sessions/.../evaluation/iter{N}/{system}/ | wc -l` — must return 8. If not, re-dispatch the evaluator with explicit Bash-write instructions.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:45:- `ls sessions/.../evaluation/iter{N}/{system}/` shows fewer than 8 files (or only `overall.md`)
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:46:- Evaluator self-narrates a directive conflict like "I should NOT write report .md files, but the brief requires per-perspective files... I'll return inline."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:50:- T05 iter1 Claude eval: agent ID `a0bf7024f118377d1` returned verdict + inline findings; `ls execution/T5/evaluation/iter1/claude/` after dispatch showed 0 files (manager later wrote proxy overall.md).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-returned-verdict-inline-no-per-perspective-files.md:51:- Comparison: prior evaluators in this session (Ideation iter1-3, Preparation iter1-3, Planning iter1-2, Execution T1-T4) all wrote 8 per-perspective files via Bash heredoc.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md:3:description: Edit tool refuses to write through symlink paths; always use the canonical worktree-absolute path for Edit operations on skill files.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md:9:session: 1b26cf20-677b-498c-8c1b-7d7e971597ac
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md:20:During Task 01 Execution, the executor attempted to edit `.claude/skills/orchestration/SKILL.md` (a symlink) using the Edit tool. The tool refused with "Refusing to write through symlink". The executor fell back to the canonical target path `.gobbi/projects/gobbi/skills/orchestration/SKILL.md` and the edit succeeded.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md:22:The Preparation iter3 edit contract states "Edit tool default = safe via either path" — this claim is empirically FALSE in this Claude Code environment.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md:43:- `execution/task-01/evaluation/iter1/claude/overall.md` — finding O-001
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md:44:- Preparation iter3 edit contract (safety-table point 1) — the doc that needs updating
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/edit-tool-refuses-symlink-paths.md:45:- Task 01 executor report: "Edit tool refused `.claude/skills/orchestration/SKILL.md` symlink; used canonical path instead"
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:9:session: 5786090e-f65a-4493-94cc-e610ce337813
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:21:During execution task P1 (features/agents prose conformance), the brief told the executor that "most docs already conform — apply a light pass" and instructed it not to churn conforming docs. Iter 1 (commit `999a403`) correctly reshaped the two deviating design docs to ADR shape and de-crypted session coordinates throughout. However, it skipped per-doc body-section checks against each type's template for docs it deemed already-shaped. References docs (4 files) were missing the required body `## Related` section; the scenario doc was missing body `**Category:**` / `**Coverage:**` / `## Related`; the shared-executor discussion was missing `## Related`. These gaps slipped through to a dual-system REVISE.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:25:Record this framing as a mistake-candidate. Every future prose-wave brief MUST instruct the executor to perform an explicit per-doc check of each document's body against its type template's complete required-section list — not just "does it look shaped."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:29:**What went wrong:** the P1 prose brief called for a light pass and discouraged churning conforming docs. This led the executor to skip explicit per-doc body-section checks against each type's template. Docs with clean-looking `##` headings were treated as conformant without verifying against the template's full required-section list.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:31:**Why:** "looks conformant at a glance" (correct frontmatter, visible `##` sections in roughly the right order) is not the same as "matches the type template's complete required-section list." References docs had `related:` frontmatter, which appeared conformant. But the references template requires a body `## Related` section between `## Insight` and `## Why it applies` — frontmatter alone is not a substitute. A brief that de-emphasizes per-doc template-section verification creates the condition for the executor to trust surface appearance.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:33:**How to recognize:** any prose/conformance brief that uses language such as "light pass," "don't touch conforming docs," or "only fix deviating docs" without also requiring an explicit per-doc check against each type template's full required-section list. Also: references or scenario docs that have the right frontmatter fields (`related:`, `category:`) but whose body sections have not been verified against the template — frontmatter keys and body sections are separate contracts.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:35:**Corrected approach:** every prose-wave brief MUST include an instruction equivalent to: "For every doc in scope, verify the body against the complete required-section list in its type template — not just whether sections are present at a glance. Specifically: references docs require a body `## Related` section between `## Insight` and `## Why it applies` (frontmatter `related:` alone is not sufficient); scenario docs require body `**Category:**` and `**Coverage:**` fields before `## Situation` and a `## Related` section; discussions docs require a body `## Related` section. Do not skip this check for docs that appear shaped — the gap is invisible without template comparison." Link to related mistakes: `[[evaluator-false-pass-without-diffing]]` and `[[conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape]]`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:39:- Accept it as a one-off executor error, not a brief-design mistake. Rejected: the brief's "light pass" framing directly invited skipping the per-doc template check; the error is structural, not accidental.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/prose-brief-light-pass-undersold-template-section-checks.md:45:- The mistake-candidate should be promoted to `features/project-memory/mistakes/` (feature-scoped, since it is specific to prose-wave execution under this feature).
