.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:3:description: "Manager ran `rm -rf` on a worktree .gobbi/ chain without verifying tracked files; deleted tracked files visible only via `git status`."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:9:session: bac669ad-4fec-40b5-8387-51ac57bc0d3d
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:10:tags: [process, worktree, git, iron-law-1]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:17:# Manager rm -rf'd worktree .gobbi/ chain without investigating tracked files
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:21:During cleanup of the Codex session-write-path violation (see companion mistake `codex-eval-session-write-path-nested-in-worktree`), the manager ran `rm -rf` on the `.gobbi/` directory chain inside the Execution worktree (`.gobbi/projects/gobbi/worktrees/feat/env-var-audit-sessionstart-hook/.gobbi/`) to remove the misplaced session files.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:23:The `.gobbi/` chain at that location was not just a stale sandbox artifact — it also contained **tracked files** that belonged to the branch HEAD (the worktree was on `feat/env-var-audit-sessionstart-hook`, and some `.gobbi/` content was committed on that branch). The `rm -rf` silently deleted tracked files without a warning, because `rm -rf` does not consult git. The manager only discovered the deletion when the subsequent `git status` showed the tracked files as deleted.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:25:Recovery was performed via `git restore .gobbi/` in the worktree, which re-checked-out the tracked files from HEAD. No permanent data loss occurred. The incident was recorded in the Preparation decisions log (finding γ adjunct note).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:31:The manager's mental model was: "this `.gobbi/` tree inside the worktree is an artifact of the Codex sandbox's incorrect write — it shouldn't be there, so I can safely remove it." The assumption missed that a git worktree shares the same `.git` tracking as the main tree. Files committed on the branch are present in the worktree by design — the worktree IS a checked-out branch. A `rm -rf` inside a worktree can delete tracked files just as easily as inside the main tree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:37:Before any `rm -rf` on a path inside a git repository (main tree or worktree):
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:39:1. **Run `git -C <worktree-root> status --short <path>`** — if the path shows any tracked files (no `??` prefix), those files are tracked and `rm -rf` will produce deleted entries in the index.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:40:2. **Run `git -C <worktree-root> ls-files --error-unmatch <path>` (or `git ls-files <path>`)** to enumerate exactly which files inside the path are tracked.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-rm-rf-without-investigating-tracked-files.md:47:- You are about to run `rm -rf <path>` on any path inside a git worktree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:9:session: 5786090e-f65a-4493-94cc-e610ce337813
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:10:tags: [process, evaluation, dual-system]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:21:On P5b (prose wave — 20 docs in `features/install-runtime/`), the dual-system evaluation produced a split verdict: Claude PASS / Codex REVISE with 2 High findings. The pessimistic-union default would treat any REVISE as blocking and trigger a remediation round.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:23:Both Codex findings, when ground-truthed against `memorization/rules.md` §4.3 (line 186) and the checklist template, directly contradicted the locked §4 standard:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:26:- **F2 (precise session-coordinate refs "weakened" to generic prose):** §4.3 mandates stripping iteration markers (`iter1`, `draft-iter3`, evaluation path pointers) from evergreen bodies and routing provenance to a `## Source` footer — exactly what P5b did. Re-inserting `evaluation/iter1/claude/structure.md` pointers would be a §4.3 violation.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:38:Dual-system evaluation provides real value (Codex caught genuine issues on P4) but the evaluator operates against the brief, not always the full standard. The brief cannot carry the full §4 standard verbatim; evaluators can over-apply a quoted clause beyond its actual scope.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:42:- **Remediate per Codex findings:** Would strip §4.3-sanctioned `## Source` footers and re-insert session-coordinate paths §4.3 mandates removing — net conformance regression. Rejected.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:48:- The manager MUST ground-truth every divergent evaluator finding against the LOCKED STANDARD, not just the evaluation brief, before deciding whether to remediate or dispute.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:50:- The dispute record MUST cite the specific standard section + line number + precedent evidence so the rationale is reproducible.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:51:- Dual-system evaluation value is preserved: the pattern (Codex catches real issues, Claude catches others) is real; the manager arbitrates divergence against the spec.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:69:4. If yes: disposition `disputed`, cite the standard + line number + precedent, do NOT remediate.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:74:- [[evaluator-false-pass-without-diffing]] — the complementary failure: evaluator claims PASS without evidence. This mistake is the REVISE-direction failure: evaluator claims REVISE when the standard says PASS.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/evaluator-revise-may-contradict-the-standard-manager-disputes-with-evidence.md:75:- `sessions/2026-05-27-5786090e-f65a-4493-94cc-e610ce337813/execution/P5b-install-runtime-b-prose/artifacts/verification-report.md` — the P5b dispute record with full citations.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:9:session: 45388fa9-74a5-42ff-acdf-1308ca35523f
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:10:tags: [process, evaluation, iron-law-7]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:20:The HANDOFF (wrap-up artifacts) recorded "both PASS" for T07 iter2, stating that both the Claude and Codex evaluation systems passed in iteration 2. The on-disk Codex evaluation artifact at `execution/task-07/evaluation/iter2/codex/overall.md` carries `VERDICT: REVISE` (OVERALL-001, High/90 — stale `gobbi/SKILL.md` refs). The Codex REVISE was a real finding that was dispositioned DEFERRED as out-of-contract during the session. The handoff flattened the "PASS on contracted scope with deferred out-of-contract REVISE" decision into a simple "both PASS" — a false summary of the on-disk eval state.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:24:The verdict carried from working memory: the manager/author held a "T07 accepted PASS on contracted scope" decision in context and wrote "both PASS" to the handoff without re-reading the on-disk evaluation files to verify the exact verdicts. The per-task PASS-on-contracted-scope decision compressed correctly in the manager's understanding, but the compression was applied to the handoff claim without verifying that the on-disk artifact matched. The on-disk Codex verdict was REVISE, not PASS. This is the same Principle 7 violation pattern as `leader-iter2-verification-claim-without-evidence` — appearing at the manager/handoff level rather than the leader/remediation level.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:28:1. Before writing any verdict into a handoff, journal, or state claim for a task iteration, `grep` the on-disk `evaluation/iterN/{system}/overall.md` VERDICT line and quote it verbatim.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:29:2. If the on-disk verdict is REVISE but the task was accepted as PASS on contracted scope, state it precisely: "Codex iter2 VERDICT: REVISE (OVERALL-001, High/90, out-of-contract) — dispositioned DEFERRED to follow-up backlog; task accepted PASS on contracted scope."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:30:3. Never flatten a nuanced disposition ("PASS on contracted scope / REVISE out-of-contract") to a bare "both PASS" — the distinction is load-bearing for the next session's understanding of open follow-up items.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:31:4. The verification procedure is the same as Principle 7's five-step gate: identify the proof artifact (on-disk overall.md), read it freshly, read the full VERDICT line, verify it matches the claim, only then write the claim.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:35:Trigger signal: a handoff, summary, or state file records a verdict (PASS, REVISE, FAIL) for a task iteration, but the claim was not preceded by a `Read` or `grep` of the on-disk `evaluation/iterN/{system}/overall.md` VERDICT line. Any verdict written from working memory is suspect.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:37:Second signal: the session's execution state carries a nuanced disposition ("PASS on contracted scope" + "deferred REVISE out-of-contract") but the handoff records only the simpler half ("both PASS") without preserving the nuance. The deferred REVISE is a distinct fact that next-session needs to know.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/handoff-verdict-claim-not-matched-to-on-disk-eval.md:39:Third signal: Wrap-up evaluation (or any reviewer) finds that the handoff claims "PASS" while the on-disk evaluation file is greppable as `VERDICT: REVISE`. The discrepancy is mechanically detectable.
