.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:3:description: Both Claude and Codex evaluator subagents wrote session EVAL artifacts to the main tree instead of the worktree path.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:9:session: 45388fa9-74a5-42ff-acdf-1308ca35523f
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:10:tags: [dual-system, worktree, evaluation, session-lifecycle, subagent-dispatch]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:17:# Evaluator subagents (Claude + Codex) write session EVAL artifacts to main tree instead of worktree
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:21:During Planning iter1 EVAL and T02 EVAL (execution), **both** the Codex evaluator leg and the Claude evaluator leg wrote their artifact files (`overall.md`, perspective files) to the **main-tree** session path (`<main>/.gobbi/projects/gobbi/sessions/.../evaluation/iter1/{claude,codex}/`) rather than the worktree-rooted equivalent.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:23:**Planning iter1 (Codex leg):** The Codex evaluator subagent invoked `codex exec` from its claude-code CWD (the main tree). Codex wrote 4 artifact files to the main-tree path. The delegation prompt cited the worktree absolute path but the `<worktreePath>` macro was documentation syntax — Codex read it literally and fell back to its CWD-rooted resolution.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:25:**T02 EVAL (both Claude + Codex legs):** The Claude evaluator also wrote to the main tree, not just Codex. Both legs' eval artifacts landed at `<main>/.gobbi/.../execution/task-02/evaluation/iter1/{claude,codex}/`. The manager had to relocate all files into the worktree post-hoc.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:27:The manager moved the files in both cases and the per-iter commit caught the corrected locations. Earlier iters (Ideation iter2–5 EVAL, Preparation EVAL Codex) did NOT exhibit this — they wrote to the worktree correctly because the manager pre-created eval output dirs at the worktree path before dispatching.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:35:All evaluator subagents (both Claude and Codex legs) run in their own claude-code process, whose default working directory is the main-tree root — the directory where the gobbi session started. When they write files, they resolve paths relative to their CWD.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:37:For the Codex leg specifically: `codex exec` inherits the subprocess CWD from the wrapping claude-code agent. Even when the delegation prompt cites worktree absolute paths, Codex's tool calls may resolve relative to its CWD when constructing parent directories — especially when the prompt uses `<worktreePath>` as a documentation macro (not a shell variable Codex resolves). Codex sees the literal string `<worktreePath>` and falls back to its CWD.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:39:For the Claude evaluator leg: the same CWD problem applies. Claude's file-writing tool calls in a spawned subagent use the subagent's own CWD (the main tree), not the worktree path, unless the subagent explicitly `cd`s to the worktree or the manager passes literal absolute paths.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:41:Root cause in both cases: the manager's delegation prompt did not instruct the subagent to `cd` to the worktree, and used `<worktreePath>` macro placeholders rather than literal absolute paths. The subagent then resolved all relative path components against its default CWD (main tree).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:45:When dispatching any evaluator subagent (Claude or Codex leg) in a `worktree-pr` session:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:47:1. **Pass the worktree absolute path as a literal string in the prompt** — not as a `<worktreePath>` macro placeholder. Subagents do not substitute macros; they read them literally.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:48:2. **Instruct the subagent to `cd <literal-worktree-path>` as the first action** before writing any files, so all path resolution is relative to the worktree root.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:49:3. **Pre-create the output directory at the worktree path** before invoking the evaluator (the manager already does this in some cases; make it mandatory).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:50:4. **Post-verify** file location by `ls`-ing the worktree eval dir after the evaluator returns. If files are absent, grep the main-tree equivalent path. If found there, `mv` them into the worktree and continue — the per-iter commit will pick up the corrected locations.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:52:For the Codex leg specifically: replace every `<worktreePath>/...` macro in the prompt with the literal resolved absolute path string. Add an explicit `cd <absolute-worktree-path>` before the `codex exec` call in the wrapper subagent's brief.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:58:- You are in `worktree-pr` mode and about to dispatch an evaluator subagent.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:59:- The delegation prompt uses `<worktreePath>` as a placeholder rather than the literal absolute path.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:60:- The delegation prompt does not include an explicit `cd <worktree-path>` instruction as the subagent's first action.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:62:After evaluator returns: check that `ls <worktree-path>/.gobbi/projects/gobbi/sessions/<sid>/{loop}/evaluation/iter{n}/{claude,codex}/` shows the expected files. If empty, run `find <main-tree> -name 'overall.md' -newer <recent-timestamp>` to locate the stray files.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:66:- `session-dir-placed-outside-worktree.md` — the parent mistake; both originate from subagents defaulting to main-tree CWD when worktree-pr mode is active.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md:67:- `qualified-git-write-path-rule.md` in `features/git-workflow/design/` — the qualified absolute-root rule that both mistakes violate.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:3:description: An executor's git add/commit (T9c) ran in the MAIN tree (cwd-reset) and committed the ENTIRE task to develop instead of the worktree chore branch. Caught by dual-eval (Claude wrong-branch FAIL) + manager git-verify; remediated by resetting develop and re-running in the worktree. Executors must run ALL git ops via `git -C <worktree-abs>` and verify branch before committing.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:9:session: b0a0eaf9-03f7-4dce-a040-c7443653a459
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:10:tags: [process, worktree, cwd-reset, git, executor, wrong-branch]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:21:During T9c (conform project-tier remainder), an executor's `git add` and `git commit` ran in the MAIN tree because the shell cwd had reset between Bash calls. The entire task's changes were committed onto the main tree's `develop` branch instead of the worktree's `chore/session-2026-05-25-a10c82d6` branch. The worktree branch was missing T9c's changes; `develop` was unexpectedly one commit ahead of `origin/develop`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:23:Detection: the Claude evaluator flagged "commit not in the expected branch lineage" (FAIL finding). The Codex evaluator independently confirmed wrong-branch commit. Manager git-verified: `git -C <worktree> log` showed T9c commit absent; `git -C <main-tree> log develop` showed it present. Remediation: `git -C <main-tree> reset --hard origin/develop` (safe — not yet pushed) + re-ran T9c entirely in the worktree via `git -C <worktree>`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:27:The executor's Bash invocations for `git add` and `git commit` did not use `git -C <worktree-absolute-path>`. After a cwd reset between calls, the `git` commands ran against whatever the shell's cwd resolved to — the main tree. The executor's prior edit operations may have landed in the worktree (via absolute Write paths), but the git ops targeted the main tree, creating a split: edits in worktree, commit on develop.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:31:Executors run ALL git operations via `git -C <worktree-abs-path>` — never bare `git`:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:33:1. Every `git add`, `git commit`, `git status`, `git log` call in an executor's Bash uses `git -C <literal-absolute-worktree-path>`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:34:2. Before any `git commit`, the executor MUST verify the branch: `git -C <worktree> rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` and confirm it equals the chore branch name. Abort if it says `develop` or anything unexpected.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:35:3. Manager delegates with the worktree path as a literal string (never a macro) and mandates the `git -C $WT` pattern explicitly in the delegation prompt.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:36:4. Post-task, manager independently verifies: `git -C <worktree> log --oneline -3` must include the task's commit; `git -C <main-tree> log develop --oneline -1` must NOT include it.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:38:Recovery: if the commit lands on develop and has NOT been pushed, `git -C <main-tree> reset --hard origin/develop` removes it safely; then re-run the task in the worktree. If already pushed: do NOT revert on develop; cherry-pick to the worktree branch and coordinate with the user before touching develop history.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:42:- The worktree branch is missing the task's expected changes after the executor reports success.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:44:- An evaluator flags "commit not an ancestor of HEAD / wrong lineage" or "branch at unexpected sha."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:45:- `git -C <worktree> log --oneline -3` does not include the task's commit message; `git -C <main-tree> log develop --oneline -3` does.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch.md:49:- [[subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree]] — the session-file-write variant of the same cwd-reset root cause (less severe: files misplaced, not a wrong-branch commit).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:3:description: A mechanical-conformance executor (T5) reshaped backlog body sections to the §4.2 per-type section contract — prose-wave work — during the conformance pass. Conformance prompts must explicitly forbid §4.2 body-section restructuring; section reshaping belongs to the prose wave only.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:9:session: b0a0eaf9-03f7-4dce-a040-c7443653a459
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:10:tags: [process, conformance, scope-creep, prose-wave, executor]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:21:During T5 (conform features/guardrails), the mechanical-conformance executor reshaped backlog body sections to match the §4.2 per-type section contract: it renamed and reordered `##` headings inside backlog files to fit the prescribed structure. This was prose-wave work (P-tasks), not conformance-wave work. The conformance pass scope is: frontmatter S-set stripping, concept-first title normalization, and type-aware KEEP key preservation. Body section restructuring — renaming/reordering `##` headings to match the §4.2 per-type contract — is explicitly deferred to the prose wave.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:23:The T5 evaluators caught the scope overstep and returned REVISE. The executor had conflated "apply §4 standard" with "apply all of §4," when the conformance pass only covers §4.4/§4.5, not §4.2.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:27:The conformance prompt said "conform to the dev-doc standard §4" without explicitly carving out the body-section contract (§4.2) as out-of-scope for the conformance pass. The executor, seeing a standard with multiple sub-sections, applied what it could — including the body-section contract. "De-crypt + conform" naturally creeps into prose-restructuring when the prompt does not bound the scope to frontmatter + inline coords.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:31:Conformance prompts must explicitly state in the scope boundary:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:33:> "DO NOT reshape body sections. The conformance pass touches: (1) frontmatter S-set stripping, (2) concept-first title normalization, (3) type-aware KEEP key preservation. The §4.2 per-type section contract (body section structure/headings) is prose-wave scope — do not rename, reorder, or add `##` headings during conformance."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:35:The prose wave (P1–P7b tasks) is the sole executor of §4.2 body-section restructuring. Any conformance commit that touches `##` heading names or order inside file bodies is out-of-scope and must be reverted.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:39:- A conformance-wave commit changes body SECTION structure: `##` headings are renamed or reordered, not just frontmatter keys removed/preserved.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:40:- The commit diff shows new or differently named `##` sections inside files where the prior structure was different — beyond just title-line edits.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/conformance-executor-pre-executed-prose-wave-reshape.md:41:- Evaluators flag "scope overstep" or "prose-wave work done in conformance pass."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:3:description: Evaluators who re-run a command with a flawed predicate confirm only that the command is deterministic — not that its predicate matches its declared scope. Multiple evaluators agreeing on a metric derived from one shared command does not verify the metric is correct.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:9:session: b0a0eaf9-03f7-4dce-a040-c7443653a459
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:10:tags: [process, evaluation, metrics, predicates]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:21:The Ideation counts (208 files / 191 content docs / 59 leak files) passed TWO consecutive dual-system evaluation rounds without correction. The `find` predicate used to produce those counts included `-not -path "*/agents/*"`, which wrongly excluded the entire in-scope `features/agents/` capability feature (14 docs, 4 leak files). The true baseline was 222 / 204 / 63. Both evaluators "reproduced" the numbers by re-running or reasoning from the same predicate — they matched each other but not reality.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:23:Planning caught the error only by reasoning about scope-vs-filter: `features/agents/` is a named in-scope feature but the predicate excluded it. The count correction was NOT a scope change.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:33:1. **Audit the predicate** against the declared scope: for every exclusion in the command, ask "does this exclusion match the Out-of-Scope declaration exactly, or could it also exclude in-scope items?"
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:34:2. **Cross-check with an independently-constructed query**: derive a second command from the scope declaration without looking at the first command, then compare outputs.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:35:3. **Enumerate the exclusion set**: for any `find` or `grep` command with `-not -path`, enumerate the dirs that the exclusion actually matches and verify they are all out-of-scope.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:41:Any metric or count defined by a command whose exclusion set or filter predicate has not been independently audited against the declared scope. Specific signals:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:44:- The command contains `-not -path`, `-exclude`, `--include`, or `| grep -v` clauses whose scope boundary has not been cross-checked against the declared in-scope set.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/reproducing-a-bugged-command-is-not-validation.md:45:- The metric's denominator is a file-set claim ("222 docs in scope") where the count command's exclusion list was not audited for inadvertent exclusion of in-scope items.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/naming-standard-needs-positive-guidance-not-just-blocklist.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/naming-standard-needs-positive-guidance-not-just-blocklist.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/naming-standard-needs-positive-guidance-not-just-blocklist.md:9:session: a10c82d6-f4c4-4ee5-a3dc-9fb7ce3815e7
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/naming-standard-needs-positive-guidance-not-just-blocklist.md:20:The memory-redesign shipped a slug naming standard (`memorization/rules.md` §1) expressed as a **forbidden-pattern blocklist** (phase prefixes, positional indices). When the blocklist proved over-broad (it failed on legitimate content words like `-decisions`), it was **softened to a non-enforced preference** — but nothing positive replaced it. Result: the standard told agents what NOT to do (and then stopped even doing that), but never taught what a GOOD name IS. Cryptic, position-encoding names (`task-01`, `d-1-worktree-row-5-5`, `row-5-5`, `2026-05-23-main`, `reframing-1-3`) kept being created and survived the migration. The user had to point out the names were still "garbage information" to a reader.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/naming-standard-needs-positive-guidance-not-just-blocklist.md:28:- Anchor the discipline in a principle (here: Principle 13's Create-operation facet) that POINTS to the standard's examples — don't rely on a mechanical regex gate.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:2:name: subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:3:description: Subagents writing session files (rawdata/eval) via RELATIVE paths after cwd-reset land them in the MAIN tree, not the worktree. Bash cwd resets to main tree between calls; relative Write/output paths resolve there. Use ABSOLUTE worktree paths for ALL session writes.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:9:session: b0a0eaf9-03f7-4dce-a040-c7443653a459
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:10:tags: [process, worktree, cwd-reset, subagent, absolute-paths]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:17:# Subagent relative-path writes stray to main tree after cwd reset
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:21:During this session, subagents delegated to write session files (rawdata/, evaluation/) used paths that were relative to their working directory. Between Bash tool calls, the shell cwd resets to the main tree root (not the worktree). Relative paths resolved against the main-tree cwd, so files intended for `<worktree>/.gobbi/projects/gobbi/sessions/<sid>/...` landed instead at `<main-tree>/.gobbi/projects/gobbi/sessions/<sid>/...`. The worktree session directory was missing the expected files; the main-tree session directory had them. The manager had to locate and consolidate the strays into the worktree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:25:Subagents assume their write-path context persists from the delegation prompt's description of the worktree. It does not. Every Bash call starts at the session's default cwd (main tree root). A subagent that constructs session write paths without an explicit `cd <worktree>` or absolute path prefix will write to the main tree whenever the path is relative — even if the delegation prompt correctly described the worktree location.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:29:Subagents use ABSOLUTE worktree paths for ALL session writes — no relative paths anywhere in session write operations:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:31:1. Manager includes the literal absolute worktree path in the delegation prompt's write-surface section, e.g.: `WT="/playinganalytics/git/gobbi/.gobbi/projects/gobbi/worktrees/<branch>"`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:32:2. Subagents expand `$WT` to the literal absolute path and prefix every Write/Edit/Bash output path with it.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:33:3. If the subagent must run Bash, the FIRST action is `cd "$WT"` before any file operation — not a note, an action.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:34:4. Manager runs post-WORK verification: `ls <worktree>/sessions/<sid>/<loop>/rawdata/` to confirm files landed in the right tree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:36:Recovery when strays occur: `mv` or `cp` stray files from main tree to worktree; do NOT commit them from the main tree. See also [[sendmessage-continued-cwd-resets-to-main-tree]] and [[executor-cwd-reset-commits-task-to-wrong-branch]] — the same cwd-reset root cause at different severity levels.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:40:- Session files appear under the main-tree `.gobbi/.../sessions/` tree rather than the worktree's equivalent path.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:41:- The worktree session dir is missing expected rawdata or eval files after a subagent returns.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:42:- `find <main-tree> -path "*/sessions/<sid>/*" -newer <timestamp>` turns up files that should be in the worktree.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-path-write-strays-to-main-tree.md:46:- [[sendmessage-continued-cwd-resets-to-main-tree]] — realized version of this mistake (commit landed on wrong branch).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:4:type: mistakes
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:5:scope: project
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:9:session: 7ea62d36-e826-4ce6-9e90-9e948007b068
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:21:The iter2 Preparation leader brief listed 8 H2 sections that contradicted the locked Idea Design A at `ideation/staging/design/item-a-codex-skill-structure.md` lines 15-23. Specifically:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:25:- The brief instructed frontmatter key `when-to-load` — but the canonical project skill convention is `allowed-tools` (16 of 16 existing skills use `allowed-tools`; 0 use `when-to-load`).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:27:The leader followed the brief faithfully. The resulting iter2 stub diverged from the locked Idea contract at exactly 2 structural points (section 7 substitution + frontmatter shape), causing REVISE verdicts from both Claude and Codex evaluators.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:31:The manager constructed the iter2 revision brief from memory — recalling "the locked 8 sections" imperfectly — rather than `Read`-ing `item-a-codex-skill-structure.md` lines 15-23 into context before authoring. The mistake originates from the iter1 Codex evaluation itself, which also miscalled `when-to-load` as the correct frontmatter. The manager inherited that incorrect finding and propagated it as a "fix-mandate" in the iter2 brief without cross-verifying against the Ideation contract.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:33:This is an Iron Law 7 (Principle 7) violation: "NO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE." The verification gate was applied to the leader's output (evaluators checked the stub against the spec) but not to the manager's own brief-construction step. The manager assumed memory was sufficient for encoding a "paste-inline-verbatim" specification.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:37:The manager MUST `Read` the locked spec into context immediately before constructing any "paste-inline-verbatim" section of a delegation brief. The `Read` call serves as the verification gate — without it, the brief is a memory claim, not a verified specification.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:39:Companion rule: when a delegation brief includes any verbatim content claim, the brief-construction turn should grep-cite the source line range (e.g., "per `item-a-codex-skill-structure.md:15-23`") and that grep must have run in the current session. A citation that was not freshly verified is not a citation — it is a paraphrase.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:41:Concrete fix for this mistake class:
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:42:1. Before authoring the revision brief, `Read` the locked spec file at the cited line range.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:44:3. Include the file path + line range as an inline citation in the brief so evaluators can re-verify independently.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:48:Before the manager authors any delegation brief that contains "exact" or "verbatim" instructions (e.g., "use exactly these 8 H2 sections in this order"), check whether the locked spec was `Read` into context within the last 10 manager turns of the current session. If the spec has not been freshly read, that is the warning sign.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-iter2-brief-failed-iron-law-7-verbatim-spec-recheck.md:54:- The brief references line numbers but those lines have not been scrolled through in-session
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-write-paths-stray-cd-doesnt-persist.md:2:name: subagent-relative-write-paths-stray-cd-doesnt-persist
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/subagent-relative-write-paths-stray-cd-doesnt-persist.md:3:description: Subagents that `cd` to the worktree and then call the Write tool with RELATIVE paths still write to the MAIN tree, because `cd` does not persist across Claude Code tool boundaries. The cd-first instruction + a relative path is NOT sufficient; absolute worktree paths in Write are required. Discovered after 4 recurrences in one session despite explicit cd-first briefs.
